<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-row-reduction" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Row Reduction Algorithm</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We now have all the pieces: elementary row operations preserve the solution set, and
      echelon forms make the solution set easy to read off. The row reduction algorithm
      ties them together into a procedure that solves <em>any</em> system of linear
      equations.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-the-algorithm">
    <title>The Algorithm</title>

    <algorithm xml:id="alg-row-reduction">
      <title>The Row Reduction Algorithm</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Identify the pivot column.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Choose a nonzero entry in the pivot column and (if necessary) move it to
                the pivot position.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Create zeros below the pivot using elementary row operations.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Repeat steps 1<ndash />3 for the sub-matrices obtained by ignoring the
                column corresponding to the previous pivot position and all the rows above
                it (if any).
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Beginning with the rightmost pivot, create zeros above the pivots and make
                the pivot <m>1</m> (if necessary).
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>

    <remark xml:id="rem-forward-backward">
      <p>
        Note that steps 1<ndash />4 will produce a row-echelon matrix, and step 5 will
        convert it to a reduced row-echelon matrix:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Steps 1<ndash />4:</term> the <term>forward phase</term>, to obtain
              the REM.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Step 5:</term> the <term>backward phase</term>, to obtain the RREM.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-three-outcomes">
    <title>The Three Possible Outcomes</title>

    <p>
      The three graphical possibilities of
      <xref ref="fig-three-possibilities" text="type-global" /> correspond to three
      outcomes of the algorithm. The next three examples illustrate each in turn.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="eg-unique-solution">
      <title>A System with a Unique Solution</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the system
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x + y + 2z \amp = -1
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              y + 2x + 3z \amp = 0
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              z - 2y \amp = 2
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First rewrite each equation with the variables in the order <m>x, y, z</m> and
          form the augmented matrix. The forward phase gives
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
                2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -2 \amp 1 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_1 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp -2 \amp 1 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp -2
              \end{array} \right],
            </mrow>
          </md>
          which is a row-echelon matrix.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now the backward phase:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{\frac{1}{3} r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{2}{3}
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow[-2r_3 + r_1]{r_3 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{1}{3} \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{4}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{2}{3}
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{1}{3} \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -\frac{4}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{2}{3}
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-r_2 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \frac{5}{3} \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -\frac{4}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{2}{3}
              \end{array} \right],
            </mrow>
          </md>
          a reduced row-echelon matrix.
        </p>
        <p>
          Reading off the rows,
          <md>
            x = \frac{5}{3}, \qquad y = -\frac{4}{3}, \qquad z = -\frac{2}{3},
          </md>
          a unique solution. Every column of the coefficient matrix is a pivot column, so
          there are no free variables.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-unique-interactive">
          <caption>
            The same system as a matrix transformation
            <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>. The violet solution set of
            <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m> in the left pane is a single <em>point</em>:
            exactly one input produces this output. Click and drag the head of
            <m>\vec{b}</m> in the right pane <mdash /> the solution point moves, but it
            stays a single point, because every column of <m>A</m> is a pivot column.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-unique-solution" iframe="demos-manim/axb.html?mat=1,1,2:2,1,3:0,-2,1&amp;x=1.667,-1.333,-0.667&amp;show=true&amp;theme=dark" width="100%" aspect="5:4"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-no-solution-rr">
      <title>An Inconsistent System</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the system
          <md>
            <mrow>
              -3x_1 - 9x_2 + x_3 \amp = -9
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              2x_1 + 6x_2 - x_3 \amp = 6
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_1 + 3x_2 - x_3 \amp = 2
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                -3 \amp -9 \amp 1 \amp -9 \\
                2 \amp 6 \amp -1 \amp 6 \\
                1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow{r_3 \leftrightarrow r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                2 \amp 6 \amp -1 \amp 6 \\
                -3 \amp -9 \amp 1 \amp -9
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow[3r_1 + r_3]{-2r_1 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp -3
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{2r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The last row says
          <md>
            0x_1 + 0x_2 + 0x_3 = 1, \qquad \text{or} \qquad 0 = 1,
          </md>
          which is impossible, and hence the system is inconsistent (no solution).
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-no-solution-interactive">
          <caption>
            Here the first two columns of <m>A</m> are multiples of one another, so the
            range of <m>T</m> (teal, right pane) is only a <em>plane</em> inside
            <m>\R^3</m>. Click and drag the head of <m>\vec{b}</m> off that plane: the
            equation is marked inconsistent and the solution set vanishes, which is what
            the row <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>
            records above.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-no-solution" iframe="demos-manim/axb.html?mat=-3,-9,1:2,6,-1:1,3,-1&amp;x=1,0,1&amp;show=true&amp;theme=dark" width="100%" aspect="5:4"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="rem-inconsistency-test">
      <p>
        In general, a system is inconsistent exactly when the row-echelon form of its
        augmented matrix has a row of the form
        <m>\left[ \begin{array}{cccc|c} 0 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \amp d \end{array} \right]</m>
        with <m>d \neq 0</m>.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <example xml:id="eg-infinitely-many-rr">
      <title>A System with Infinitely Many Solutions</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the system
          <md>
            <mrow>
              2x + y + 3z \amp = 1
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              2y - z + x \amp = 0
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              10z - 6y \amp = 2
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \amp
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -6 \amp 10 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{r_1 \leftrightarrow r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -6 \amp 10 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_1 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -3 \amp 5 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -6 \amp 10 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -3 \amp 5 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-\frac{1}{3} r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{5}{3} \amp -\frac{1}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_2 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{7}{3} \amp \frac{2}{3} \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{5}{3} \amp -\frac{1}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Columns <m>1</m> and <m>2</m> are pivot columns, so <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are
          basic variables, whereas <m>z</m> is a free variable. Setting <m>z = s</m>, the
          two nonzero rows read
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x + \tfrac{7}{3}s \amp = \tfrac{2}{3}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              y - \tfrac{5}{3}s \amp = -\tfrac{1}{3}
            </mrow>
          </md>
          and therefore the solution set in parametric form is
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x \amp = \tfrac{2}{3} - \tfrac{7}{3}s
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              y \amp = -\tfrac{1}{3} + \tfrac{5}{3}s
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              z \amp = s, \qquad \text{where } s \in \R.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-infinitely-many-interactive">
          <caption>
            With one free variable, the violet solution set in the left pane is a
            <em>line</em> rather than a point: every input on that line has the same
            image. Click and drag the head of <m>\vec{x}</m> along the line and watch
            <m>\vec{b}</m> stay put; drag <m>\vec{b}</m> instead and the whole solution
            line moves with it.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-infinitely-many" iframe="demos-manim/axb.html?mat=2,1,3:1,2,-1:0,-6,10&amp;x=0.667,-0.333,0&amp;show=true&amp;theme=dark" width="100%" aspect="5:4"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="rem-free-variables-count">
      <p>
        The pattern is now clear. If a system is consistent, then it has a unique solution
        when there are no free variables, and infinitely many solutions when there is at
        least one free variable <mdash /> one parameter for each free variable.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-systems-with-parameters">
    <title>Systems Depending on Parameters</title>

    <example xml:id="eg-parameters-abc">
      <title>Conditions on the Coefficients</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find all values of <m>a</m>, <m>b</m> and <m>c</m> (or conditions on <m>a</m>,
          <m>b</m> and <m>c</m>) so that the system
          <md>
            <mrow>
              2x + 3y + az \amp = b
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \phantom{2x + {}} -y + 2z \amp = c
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x + 3y - 2z \amp = 1
            </mrow>
          </md>
          has (i) a unique solution, (ii) no solutions, and (iii) infinitely many
          solutions. In (i) and (iii), find the solution(s).
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Row reduce the augmented matrix, treating <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m> as
          constants:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                2 \amp 3 \amp a \amp b \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow{r_1 \leftrightarrow r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                2 \amp 3 \amp a \amp b
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_1 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                0 \amp -3 \amp a+4 \amp b-2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-3r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp a-2 \amp b-2-3c
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
          Everything now hinges on the entry <m>a - 2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Case (I): <m>a - 2 \neq 0</m>, i.e., <m>a \neq 2</m>.</term>
          We may divide the third row by <m>a-2</m> and continue. Writing
          <m>K = \dfrac{b-2-3c}{a-2}</m> for brevity,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{\left( \frac{1}{a-2} \right) r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow[r_2 + 2r_3]{-r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -c + 2K \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{r_1 + 2r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp 0 \amp 1 + 2K \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -c + 2K \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-3r_2 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 + 3c - 4K \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -c + 2K \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
          Hence when <m>a \neq 2</m>, there is a unique solution:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x \amp = 1 + 3c - 4\left( \frac{b-2-3c}{a-2} \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              y \amp = -c + 2 \left( \frac{b-2-3c}{a-2} \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              z \amp = \frac{b-2-3c}{a-2}.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Case (II): <m>a - 2 = 0</m> and <m>b - 2 - 3c \neq 0</m>.</term>
          The row-echelon matrix is
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp b-2-3c
            \end{array} \right],
          </md>
          whose last row asserts <m>0 = b-2-3c \neq 0</m>. Then the system has no solution.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Case (III): <m>a - 2 = 0</m> and <m>b - 3c = 2</m>.</term>
          Now the last row is entirely zero, and <m>z</m> is a free variable, so the
          system has infinitely many solutions. Setting <m>z = s</m>, the second row gives
          <m>-y + 2s = c</m>, so <m>y = -c + 2s</m>, and the first row gives
          <md>
            x = 1 - 3y + 2z = 1 - 3(-c + 2s) + 2s = 1 + 3c - 4s.
          </md>
          The solution set in parametric form is
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x \amp = 1 + 3c - 4s
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              y \amp = -c + 2s
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              z \amp = s, \qquad \text{where } s \in \R.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
